"[2606.22636] Spectral Gap for the Binary Fixed-Margin Swap Chain (https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.22636)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 167\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Probability\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Jun 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 13 Jul 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Spectral Gap for the Binary Fixed-Margin Swap Chain\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Weibo Fu , cite7†Qian Qin , cite8†Guanyang Wang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Spectral Gap for the Binary Fixed-Margin Swap Chain, by Weibo Fu and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We prove an explicit spectral-gap lower bound for the lazy swap chain on binary matrices with prescribed row and column sums. This chain is a standard sampler for fixed-margin null models in ecology, statistics, and network analysis. Kannan, Tetali, and Vempala (KTV) conjectured that it mixes rapidly for all feasible margins \\citep{kannan1997simple}.\nL17: We show that for every feasible set of margins on an $m\\times n$ binary matrix, the lazy swap chain has spectral gap at least $$\\binom{m}{2}^{-1}\\binom{n}{2}^{-1}.$$ The bound is tight in the worst case. Thus, our result proves this KTV conjecture in a stronger quantitative form. The same spectral-gap bound also verifies the Mihail--Vazirani conjecture for fixed-margin 0/1-matrix polytopes.\nL18: > The proof gives a new route to fixed-margin sampling that avoids stability assumptions and canonical-path constructions. We compare the swap chain with a two-row heat-bath chain and use a local-to-global spectral reduction to reduce the analysis from arbitrary $m\\times n$ matrices to a three-row problem. The remaining three-row inequality is then proved by separating the scalar column-count sector from the non-scalar Johnson harmonic sectors.\nL19: > The proof itself was generated by ChatGPT 5.5 Pro. The author's role was to pose the problem, guide the search direction, evaluate the AI-generated arguments, rewrite the proof, and take responsibility for the final form and validity of the result. The full proof of the main theorem has been formalized in Lean, and the accompanying formalization is available at the anonymous repository cite11†this https URL†github.com .\nL20: Comments:  | add acorollary and additional references\nL21: Subjects:  | Probability (math.PR); Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS); Combinatorics (math.CO); Computation (stat.CO)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2606.22636 [math.PR]\nL23:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2606.22636v2 [math.PR] for this version)\nL24:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.22636†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Guanyang Wang [cite15†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Sun, 21 Jun 2026 18:46:59 UTC (19 KB)\nL29: [v2] Mon, 13 Jul 2026 03:28:59 UTC (25 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Spectral Gap for the Binary Fixed-Margin Swap Chain, by Weibo Fu and 2 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite9†View PDF L38:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite16†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: math.PR\nL45: \nL46: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L47: \nL48: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2026-06 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite23†cs L53: cite24†cs.DS L54: cite25†math L55: cite26†math.CO L56: cite27†stat L57: cite28†stat.CO L58: \nL59: ### References & Citations\nL60: \nL61:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui