day; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 168\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Combinatorics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Mar 2019 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 31 Jul 2021 (this version, v5)]\nL9: # Title:The mixing time of the switch Markov chains: a unified approach\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Péter L. Erdős , cite7†Catherine Greenhill , cite8†Tamás Róbert Mezei , cite9†István Miklós , cite10†Dániel Soltész , cite11†Lajos Soukup L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled The mixing time of the switch Markov chains: a unified approach, by P\\'eter L. Erd\\H{o}s and Catherine Greenhill and Tam\\'as R\\'obert Mezei and Istv\\'an Mikl\\'os and D\\'aniel Solt\\'esz and Lajos Soukup\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Since 1997 a considerable effort has been spent to study the mixing time of switch Markov chains on the realizations of graphic degree sequences of simple graphs. Several results were proved on rapidly mixing Markov chains on unconstrained, bipartite, and directed sequences, using different mechanisms. The aim of this paper is to unify these approaches.\nL17: We will illustrate the strength of the unified method by showing that on any $P$-stable family of unconstrained/bipartite/directed degree sequences the switch Markov chain is rapidly mixing. This is a common generalization of every known result that shows the rapid mixing nature of the switch Markov chain on a region of degree sequences. Two applications of this general result will be presented. One is an almost uniform sampler for power-law degree sequences with exponent $\\gamma>1+\\sqrt{3}$.\nL18: The other one shows that the switch Markov chain on the degree sequence of an Erdős-Rényi random graph $G(n,p)$ is asymptotically almost surely rapidly mixing if $p$ is bounded away from 0 and 1 by at least $\\frac{5\\log n}{n-1}$.\nL19: Comments:  | final review\nL20: Subjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 05C30 60J10 68R10\nL22: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:1903.06600 [math.CO]\nL23:    | (or cite15†arXiv:1903.06600v5 [math.CO] for this version)\nL24:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1903.06600†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: Journal reference:  | European Journal of Combinatorics 99 (2022) 103421\nL26: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite17†https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2021.103421†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Péter L. Erdős [cite18†view email ]\nL30: cite5†[v1] Fri, 15 Mar 2019 15:25:43 UTC (41 KB)\nL31: cite19†[v2] Tue, 19 Mar 2019 15:40:43 UTC (41 KB)\nL32: cite20†[v3] Thu, 4 Jul 2019 20:57:49 UTC (48 KB)\nL33: cite21†[v4] Tue, 5 Nov 2019 01:47:13 UTC (47 KB)\nL34: [v5] Sat, 31 Jul 2021 14:55:43 UTC (60 KB)\nL35: \nL36: Full-text links:\nL37: ## Access Paper:\nL38: \nL39: View a PDF of the paper titled The mixing time of the switch Markov chains: a unified approach, by P\\'eter L. Erd\\H{o}s and Catherine Greenhill and Tam\\'as R\\'obert Mezei and Istv\\'an Mikl\\'os and D\\'aniel Solt\\'esz and Lajos Soukup\nL40: \nL41:   * cite12†View PDF L42:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L43:   * cite22†TeX Source L44: \nL45: cite23†view license†creativecommons.org L46: ### Current browse context:\nL47: \nL48: math.CO\nL49: \nL50: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L51: \nL52: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2019-03 L53: \nL54: Change to browse by:\nL55: \nL56: cite29†cs L57: cite30†cs.DM L58: cite31†math L59: \nL60: ### References & Citations\nL61: \nL62:   * cite32†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs